{
  "id": "Compound::DB01248 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Docetaxel, a well-established microtubule inhibitor, is implicated in the treatment of breast cancer through a series of mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Docetaxel, along with its analogs Ixabepilone and Eribulin, shares a common mode of action involving microtubule inhibition, which is a key pathway in cancer cell proliferation. This inhibition is further supported by the inclusion of these compounds in the broader category of Tetracyclic Diterpenoids, which are known to interact with microtubules. The shared pathway of microtubule inhibition across these compounds provides a strong foundation for their use in treating breast cancer. The low common ancestor between Docetaxel and its analogs, as indicated by the Tetracyclic Diterpenoid category, underscores the structural and functional similarities that contribute to their therapeutic effects. The integrated evidence highlights the importance of microtubule-targeting agents in the treatment of breast cancer, supported by a consistent and well-documented mechanism of action.",
